  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Tuesday) \nIssued at 951 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nAn upper level shortwave trough looks to sweep over the Mississippi \nRiver Valley and towards the Northeastern U.S. on Saturday. By Sunday\nand through early next week, upper ridging is shown to build over \nthe southern Plains and Southeastern U.S. Meanwhile, surface high \npressure is expected to remain parked over the Appalachians for much \nof this time. This feature does shift south over the northern Gulf \ncoast by Tuesday, however. Overall, anticipating daily chances of \nshowers and storms, mainly during the afternoon and evening hours. \nExpect higher chances of showers and storms on Saturday due to the \naforementioned upper shortwave trough swinging over the region, with \nchances decreasing for Sunday through Tuesday. PWATs will remain \nelevated, but bulk shear will likely be 20 knots or less throughout \nthe period. Therefore, severe weather is not anticipated, but \nfrequent lightning and heavy rain will be the main threats with any \nstorms.\n\nAs for temperatures, highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s with lows \nin the lower to mid 70s will be common. With elevated moisture \npersisting, afternoon heat index values are forecast to mostly range \nbetween 98-104 degrees through the weekend, then increase a bit by \nearly next week as rain chances decrease. This results in a Moderate \nHeatRisk for north Alabama and Southern Middle Tennessee this \nweekend, with some locations in the Major category by Monday. This\nwill be something to watch in case Heat Advisories become \nnecessary for all or portions of the local area. Regardless, \nplease remember heat safety! Drink plenty of water, take frequent \nbreaks in the shade, and never leave people or pets in vehicles!",
